<PubmedArticle><MedlineCitation Status="MEDLINE" Owner="NLM" IndexingMethod="Automated"><PMID Version="1">41923630</PMID><DateCompleted><Year>2026</Year><Month>07</Month><Day>14</Day></DateCompleted><DateRevised><Year>2026</Year><Month>08</Month><Day>13</Day></DateRevised><Article PubModel="Print-Electronic"><Journal><ISSN IssnType="Electronic">2666-3791</ISSN><JournalIssue CitedMedium="Internet"><Volume>7</Volume><Issue>4</Issue><PubDate><Year>2026</Year><Month>Apr</Month><Day>21</Day></PubDate></JournalIssue><Title>Cell reports. Medicine</Title><ISOAbbreviation>Cell Rep Med</ISOAbbreviation></Journal><ArticleTitle>Single-cell analysis highlights the significance of malignant cell IFN/MHC-II for immunotherapy response in head and neck squamous cell carcinoma.</ArticleTitle><Pagination><StartPage>102715</StartPage><MedlinePgn>102715</MedlinePgn></Pagination><ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">102715</ELocationID><ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.xcrm.2026.102715</ELocationID><ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S2666-3791(26)00132-1</ELocationID><Abstract><AbstractText>In head and neck squamous cell carcinoma (HNSCC), immunotherapy response rates remain modest, with difficulty predicting responders. Previous studies characterizing immunotherapy-associated cellular changes in HNSCC focus on immune cells, providing limited insight into malignant cell responses. Here, we perform single-cell RNA sequencing (RNA-seq) on 16 HNSCC patients pre- and post-neoadjuvant pembrolizumab treatment. We identify an interferon (IFN)/major histocompatibility complex class II (MHC-II) expression program in malignant cells, characterized by MHC-II and IFN-response genes, which is associated with response to pembrolizumab. We validate malignant cell MHC-II expression at the protein level via multiplexed immunofluorescence. In a murine HNSCC model, IFN-&#x3b3;-induced malignant cell MHC-II expression marks immunotherapy-sensitive tumors with favorable immune microenvironments. Finally, we confirm that pre-treatment malignant-IFN/MHC-II is a marker of response through deconvolution of bulk RNA-seq data from an independent cohort. Beyond identifying the malignant IFN/MHC-II program as a potential biomarker for immunotherapy response in HNSCC, our work elucidates the important role of malignant cells in immunotherapy.</AbstractText><CopyrightInformation>Copyright &#xa9; 2026 The Authors. Published by Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.</CopyrightInformation></Abstract><AuthorList CompleteYN="Y"><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Mints</LastName><ForeName>Michael</ForeName><Initials>M</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Department of Molecular Cell Biology, Weizmann Institute of Science, Rehovot, Israel; Department of Oncology-Pathology, Karolinska Institute, Stockholm, Sweden.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Sample</LastName><ForeName>Reilly A</ForeName><Initials>RA</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Department of Otolaryngology-Head and Neck Surgery, Washington University School of Medicine, St. Louis, MO, USA; Department of Genetics, Washington University School of Medicine, St. Louis, MO, USA.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Parikh</LastName><ForeName>Anuraag S</ForeName><Initials>AS</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Department of Otolaryngology-Head and Neck Surgery, Columbia University Irving Medical Center, New York, NY, USA.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Zaretsky</LastName><ForeName>Jesse M</ForeName><Initials>JM</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Department of Medicine/Medical Oncology, Washington University School of Medicine, St. Louis, MO, USA; The Robert Ebert and Greg Stubblefield Head and Neck Tumor Center at Siteman Cancer Center at Siteman, Washington University School of Medicine, St. Louis, MO, USA.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Qi</LastName><ForeNa